+A retry has to resend the request body, so it only applies when that body can
+be rewound. Bodies the SDK encodes itself (JSON, form and multipart) always
+can. If you pass your own `io.Reader` to `Files.Add` or `Files.Update`, it is
+replayable when it is seekable — an `*os.File`, `*bytes.Reader` or
+`*strings.Reader`. A reader that cannot seek, such as an `io.Pipe` or an
+`io.MultiReader`, is sent once and the 429 or 5xx is returned to you.
+
 Response bodies are capped at 64 MiB by default. Override globally with
 `Config.MaxResponseSize`, per request with `WithResponseSizeLimit` or
 `WithNoResponseSizeLimit`, and use `client.Files.DownloadTo` to stream large
 v1 file downloads.
 
+## File uploads (v1)
+
+`Files.Upload` takes a file name and a reader and builds the
+`multipart/form-data` body for you:
+
+```go
+f, err := os.Open("contract.pdf")
+if err != nil {
+	log.Fatal(err)
+}
+defer f.Close()
+
+file, err := client.Files.Upload(
+	context.Background(),
+	"contract.pdf",
+	f,
+	v1.WithFileDealID(v1.DealID(123)),
+)
+```
+
+Associate the file with a record using `WithFileDealID`, `WithFilePersonID`,
+`WithFileOrganizationID`, `WithFileProductID`, `WithFileActivityID`,
+`WithFileProjectID` or `WithFileLeadID`, and set anything not wrapped yet with
+`WithFileFormField`. The encoded body is buffered in memory, which keeps the
+upload retryable.
+
+`Files.Add` remains available for callers that need to encode the request body
+themselves.
+
